===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[collide]] or [[strike]], the act of [[impinging]].
#: {{ux|en|When the hammer '''impacts''' the nail, it bends.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Mike Williams|title=Microbullet hits confirm graphene’s strength|magazine=w:Rice University|url=https://news2.rice.edu/2014/12/01/microbullet-hits-confirm-graphenes-strength-2/|date=2014-12-01|passage=As a microbullet '''impacts''' the graphene, the diameter of the cone it creates – determined by later examination of the petals – provides a way to measure how much energy the graphene absorbs before breaking.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260308233022/https://news2.rice.edu/2014/12/01/microbullet-hits-confirm-graphenes-strength-2/|archivedate=8 March 2026}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[compress]]; to [[compact]]; to press into something or pack together.
#: {{ux|en|The footprints of birds do not '''impact''' the soil in the way those of dinosaurs do.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|figurative|proscribed}} To [[significant]]ly or [[strong]]ly [[influence]] or [[affect]]; to have an [[impact#Noun|impact]] on.
#: {{ux|en|I can make the changes, but it will '''impact''' the schedule.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|rare}} To [[stamp]] or [[impress]] onto something.
#: {{ux|en|Ideas '''impacted''' on the mind.}}

====Usage notes====
Although most {{w|verbification}} instances in English draw no prescriptive attention, some authorities object to the verb sense of ''impact'' meaning "to significantly or strongly influence or affect; to have an impact on". To avoid controversy, one can replace the verb sense with ''[[affect]]'', which nearly always produces an acceptable result. See also [[#Usage_notes|the usage note for the noun sense]].

