====Noun====
{{wp}}
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|mathematics}} One of the two fundamental [[operations]] of [[calculus]] (the other being [[differentiation]]), whereby a [[function]]'s [[displacement]], [[area]], [[volume]], or other qualities arising from the study of [[infinitesimal]] change are quantified, usually defined as a [[limit|limiting]] process on a [[sequence]] of [[partial sum]]s. Denoted using a [[long s]]: [[∫]], or a variant thereof.
#: {{ux|en|The '''integral''' of a [[univariate ]][[real-valued]] function is the area under its curve; but be warned! Not all functions are [[integrable]]!}}
## {{lb|en|specifically}} Any of several [[analysis|analytic]] [[formalization]]s of this operation: the [[Riemann integral]], the [[Lebesgue integral]], etc.
# {{lb|en|mathematics}} A [[definite integral]]: the result of the [[application]] of such an operation onto a function and a suitable [[subset]] of the function's [[domain]]: either a [[number]] or [[positive]] or [[negative]] [[infinity]]. In the former case, the integral is said to be [[finite]] or to [[converge]]; in the latter, the integral is said to [[diverge]]. In notation, the domain of integration is indicated either below the sign, or, if it is an [[interval]], with its endpoints as sub- and super-scripts, and the function being integrated forming part of the [[integrand]] (or, generally, [[differential form]]) appearing in front of the integral sign.
#: {{ux|en|The '''integral''' of <math>\frac{1}{x}</math> on <math>[\frac{1}{2}, 1]</math> is <math>\ln(2)</math>, but the '''integral''' of the same function on <math>(0, 1]</math> diverges. In notation, <math>\int_\frac{1}{2}^1\frac{1}{x} dx = \ln(2)</math>, but <math>\int_0^1\frac{1}{x} dx = \infty</math>.}}
#: {{ux|en|Stokes' theorem relates the '''integral''' over a surface of the [[curl]] of a [[vector field]] to a [[line integral|line '''integral''']] around the boundary of that surface: <math>    \int_S \nabla\times \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{S} = \int_{\partial S} \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r},
</math>.}}
# {{lb|en|mathematics}} An [[indefinite integral]]: the result of the application of such an operation onto a function together with an [[indefinite]] domain, yielding a function; a function's [[antiderivative]];
#: {{syn|en|antiderivative|indefinite integral|mul:∫}}
#: {{ant|en|derivative}}
#: {{ux|en|The '''integral''' of <math>x^2</math> is <math>\frac{x^3}{3}</math> plus a constant.}}
## {{lb|en|mathematics|historical or obsolete}} The [[fluent]] of a given [[fluxion]] in [[Newtonian]] calculus.

