===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The tendency to work persistently.
#: {{syn|en|industriousness|diligence|hardworkingness}}
#: {{cot|en|conscientiousness|grit#Noun}}
#: {{ux|en|Over the years, their '''industry''' and business sense made them wealthy.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1941|author=Ogden Nash|chapter=The Ant|title=The Face is Familiar|publisher=Garden City Publishing Company|page=224
|passage=The ant has made himself illustrious / Through constant '''industry''' industrious. / So what? / Would you be calm and placid / If you were full of formic acid?}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1953|month=June|author=C. E. N. Watts|title=Railway Through Lapland|journal=Railway Magazine|page=384|text=The line ends at Narvik. Eight years ago the whole town was in ruins, but the '''industry''' of the Norwegian people has rebuilt it entirely.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=November 12, 2011|work=BBC Sport
|title=[http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/15693231.stm International friendly: England 1-0 Spain]
|passage=England's win was built on '''industry''' and discipline, epitomised by the performances of Manchester City's Joleon Lescott in defence and Scott Parker in midfield.}}
# {{lb|en|countable|business|economics}} Businesses of the same type, considered as a whole; [[trade]].
#: {{ux|en|The software and tourism '''industries''' continue to grow, while the steel '''industry''' remains troubled.}}
#: {{ux|en|The steel '''industry''' has long used [[blast furnace]]s to smelt iron.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2012|author=Christoper Zara|title=Tortured Artists: From Picasso and Monroe to Warhol and Winehouse, the Twisted Secrets of the World's Most Creative Minds|section=part 1, chapter 2, {{gbooks|q=industry|id=HV4jKhjzMVYC|pg=51}}
|passage=Long before popular music evolved its many genres and subgenres, the '''industry''' was driven by a simple one-size-fits-all philosophy uncomplicated by impassioned debates over the origins of trip hop or the difference between deatchore and screamo.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-06-01|volume=407|issue=8838|page=71|magazine=w:The Economist
|title=[http://www.economist.com/news/finance-and-economics/21578670-peer-peer-lending-needs-new-name-end-peer-show End of the peer show]
|passage=Finance is seldom romantic. But the idea of peer-to-peer lending comes close. This is an '''industry''' that brings together individual savers and lenders on online platforms. Those that want to borrow are matched with those that want to lend.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|economics}} Businesses that produce [[goods]] as [[opposed]] to [[services]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author=w:Edwin Black
|title=[http://openlibrary.org/works/OL4103950W Internal Combustion]|chapter=2
|passage=But through the oligopoly, charcoal fuel proliferated throughout London's trades and '''industries'''.&nbsp; By the 1200s, brewers and bakers, tilemakers, glassblowers, pottery producers, and a range of other craftsmen all became hour-to-hour consumers of charcoal.}}
# {{lb|en|in the singular|economics}} The sector of the economy consisting of large-scale enterprises.
#: {{ux|en|There used to be a lot of '''industry''' around here, but now the economy depends on tourism.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-07-20|volume=408|issue=8845|magazine=w:The Economist
|title=[http://www.economist.com/news/http://www.economist.com/news/asia/21582043-villagers-enjoy-sunlight-after-dark-out-gloom Out of the gloom]
|passage=[Rural solar plant] schemes are of little help to '''industry''' or other heavy users of electricity. Nor is solar power yet as cheap as the grid. For all that, the rapid arrival of electric light to Indian villages is long overdue. When the national grid suffers its next huge outage, as it did in July 2012 when hundreds of millions were left in the dark, look for specks of light in the villages.}}
# {{lb|en|European|_|software|_|patent law}} Automated production of material goods.
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The economics of the European patent system|author=Dominique Guellec|coauthors=Bruno van Pottelsberghe de la Potterie|year=2007|passage=It is a classical and restricted view both of '''industry''' (it excludes service sectors, now 70% of the GDP of developed economies){{...}}|page=122}}
