# The act of [[inquiring]]; a seeking of information by asking questions; [[interrogation]]; a [[question]] or questioning.
#* {{quote-book|en|url=https://www.google.com/books/edition/English_Men_of_Letters/g1w6AQAAMAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=%22flutters+of%22&pg=PA175&printsec=frontcover|title=English Men of Letters|volume=30|passage=Yet in that February there were still some flutters of song, one of which was, Hey for the Lass wi' a Tocher, written in answer to Thomson's beseeching '''inquiry''' if he was never to hear from him again.|editor=John Morley|year=1901}}
# A search for [[truth]], [[information]], or [[knowledge]]; [[examination]] of facts or principles; research; [[investigation]]; [[inquest]].
#: {{ux|en|Scientific '''inquiry'''}}
#* {{quote-web|en|date=17 July 2025|author=Oliver Holmes|work=w:The Guardian|title=Trump calls Epstein conspiracy a ‘hoax’ and turns on Maga ‘weaklings’|url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jul/16/donald-trump-dismisses-inquiry-into-jeffrey-epstein-as-boring|text=Donald Trump has lashed out against his own supporters, calling them gullible “weaklings” for questioning the transparency of a secretive government '''inquiry''' into the late high-profile socialite and sex offender Jeffrey Epstein.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250810132900/https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jul/16/donald-trump-dismisses-inquiry-into-jeffrey-epstein-as-boring}}

====Usage notes====
* The spelling {{m|en|inquiry}} is more common, especially in American English, but also in British English.<ref>[https://books.google.com/ngrams/graph?content=inquiry%2Cenquiry&year_start=1800&year_end=2019&corpus=en-GB-2019&smoothing=3 Google Books Ngram Viewer]</ref>
* The 1926 ''Fowler's Modern English Usage'' said that ''inquiry'' should be used in relation to a formal [[inquest]] and ''[[enquiry]]'' to the general act of questioning, and said that British writers maintained this distinction. The Oxford English Dictionary, in its entry not updated since 1900, lists ''inquiry'' and ''enquiry'' as equal alternatives, in that order. Some British dictionaries, such as ''[http://www.chambersharrap.co.uk/chambers/features/chref/chref.py/main?title=21st&query=inquiry Chambers 21st Century Dictionary]'', present the two spellings as interchangeable variants in the general sense, but prefer ''inquiry'' for the "formal inquest" sense. In Australian English, ''inquiry'' represents a formal inquest (such as a government investigation) while ''enquiry'' is used in the act of questioning (e.g.: the customer enquired about the status of his loan application). Both spellings are current in Canadian English, where ''enquiry'' is often associated with scholarly or intellectual research.<ref>See Pam Peters, ''The Cambridge Guide to English Usage'', p. 282.</ref> American English usually uses ''inquiry''.

