===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[bend]] or move (something) out of a given plane or direction, often the [[horizontal]] or [[vertical]].
#: {{ux|en|He had to '''incline''' his body against the gusts to avoid being blown down in the storm.}}
#: {{ux|en|The people following the coffin '''inclined''' their heads in grief.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[slope]].
#: {{ux|en|Over the centuries the wind made the walls of the farmhouse '''incline'''.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To move (someone) towards a certain attitude, course of action, point of view, etc.
#: {{syn|en|dispose}}
#: {{ux|en|Her erratic behaviour '''inclines''' me to doubt that she can be trusted.}}
#: {{ux|en|His aggressive temperament '''inclines''' me towards caution in his presence.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1794|author=w:Samuel Johnson|chapter=[[w:Thomas Parnell (poet)|Parnell]]|title=w:Lives of the Most Eminent English Poets|passage=Such notice from such a man '''inclines''' me to believe that the vice of which he has been accused was not gross, or not notorious.|volume=2|page=280|pageurl=https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Lives_of_the_Most_Eminent_English_Poets/Volume_2/Parnell}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1817|author=[[w:William Kirby (entomologist)|William Kirby]]|author2=[[w:William Spence (entomologist)|William Spence]]|title=An Introduction to Entomology: or Elements of the Natural History of Insects|volume=2|passage=Whether the insects I observed upon the beach wetted by the waves, had flown from our own shores, and falling into the water had been brought back by the tide; or whether they had succeeded in the attempt to pass from the continent to us, by flying as far as they could, and then falling had been brought by the waves, cannot certainly be ascertained; but Kalm's observation '''inclines''' me to the latter opinion.|page=13|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/b21299900_0002/page/n21/mode/2up?q=inclines}}
#* {{quote-web|en|date=1894-08-13|quotee=[[w:Robert Gascoyne-Cecil, 3rd Marquess of Salisbury|Marquess of Salisbury]]|title=Tenants Arbitration (Ireland) Bill. (No. 203.) Second reading, HL Deb 13 August 1894 vol 28 cc654-754|work=House of Lords Hansard|text=The noble Lord said Mr. Carson admitted that it was necessary to deal with those evicted tenants. My memory '''inclines''' me to doubt very much whether Mr. Carson said that.|url=https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/lords/1894/aug/13/second-reading-1#S4V0028P0_18940813_HOL_47}}
#* {{RQ:Chambers Younger Set|VIII|passage="My tastes," he said, still smiling, "'''incline''' me to the garishly sunlit side of this planet." And, to tease her and arouse her to combat: "I prefer a farandole to a nocturne; I'd rather have a painting than an etching; Mr. Whistler bores me with his monochromatic mud; I don't like dull colours, dull sounds, dull intellects;{{nb...}}."}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|or|in the passive}} To [[tend]] to do or believe something, or move or be moved in a certain direction, away from a [[point of view]], [[attitude]], etc.
#: {{ux|en|He '''inclines''' to believe anything he reads in the newspapers.|q=intransitive}}
#: {{ux|en|I'm '''inclined''' to give up smoking after hearing of the risks to my health.|q=passive voice}}
#* '''1966''', J. M. G. van der Poel, "Agriculture in Pre- and Protohistoric Times", in the ''Acta Historiae Neerlandica'' published by the Netherlands Committee of Historical Sciences, p.170:
#*: The [[terp]] farmer made use of the plough, as is shown by the discovery of three ploughshares and four [[coulter]]s.{{...}}Those who '''inclined''' to the stock-breeding theory based their arguments on the absence of ploughs,{{nb...}}.

