# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The state of being [[insusceptible]] to something; notably:
## {{lb|en|medicine}} {{cap|protective}} resistance against [[disease]].
##: {{ux|en|Some people have better '''immunity''' to diseases than others.}}
## {{lb|en|law}} An [[exemption]] from specified duties, such as payments or services.
##: {{ux|en|Feudal privileges often included tax and other '''immunities'''.}}
## {{lb|en|law}} An [[exemption]] from [[prosecution]].
##: {{ux|en|The prosecutor offered the lieutenant '''immunity''' for all the crimes he would testify having known to be planned by the elusive drug baron.}}
## {{lb|en|religion}} An [[exemption]] from [[penance]].
##* {{RQ:Landon Lady Anne Granard|page=81|volume=II|passage=Nevertheless, his reason rebelled, and his sense of religion by no means allowed the justice of refusing the amiable, handsome, and wealthy Englishman, whom his sweet child preferred, and who was perfectly willing that the signora should enjoy all the liberty required by her conscience and her church, together with such sums as should, from time to time, purchase the prayers of the faithful, and all other '''immunities''', so far as his fortune furnished the means.}}
## {{lb|en|in games and competitions}} An [[exemption]] given to a [[player]] from [[lose|losing]] or being [[withdrawn]] from play.
##: {{ux|en|After winning the last round the player was granted '''immunity''' which allowed him to stay in the game even after receiving the fewest points.}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} A [[resistance]] to a specific thing.
#: {{ux|en|Superbugs are bacteria that develop an '''immunity''' to antibiotics.}}

